;;; A string written in a rule's `:match` never matches — **W18**,
;;; regression `str-in-match` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Four `holds` facts, over the names `1` and `a` and the strings `"1"`
;;; and `"b"`. Three rules match one `holds` fact each: `s` writes the
;;; string `"1"`, `t` the name `b`, `u` the name `1`. `t` and `u` are
;;; the controls.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), written
;;; only after `:why`, `:source` or `:goal-text`: the program is refused at
;;; the parse, at its first string, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `ff023413`, in a release build and a debug one, `t` and `u` fired and
;;; `s` never did: the rule compiler did not lower a string, so in a
;;; `:match` it was an opaque slot that matched nothing (`compile.rs`'
;;; `slot`). R was the eight subsets, and no state held `(hit s)`: `ein solve
;;; FILE --minimal` printed root's state as `(model (hit t) (hit u))`, the
;;; summary's `root.facts_by_relation.hit` 2. Today the program is refused
;;; at the parse: *unexpected `"1"`: a string*.
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds 1) (holds "1") (holds a) (holds "b")
(rule s () :match (holds "1") :assert (hit s))
(rule t () :match (holds b) :assert (hit t))
(rule u () :match (holds 1) :assert (hit u))
